      "s": "CNBC Tech",
      "t": "Families wait in agony as Nepal floods leaves hundreds missing",
      "d": "A New York couple traveling to Mount Kailash is among 65 Americans missing after a catastrophic flood along the Nepal-Tibet border.",
      "u": "https://www.nbcnews.com/world/asia/nepal-tibet-floods-leave-hundreds-missing-65-americans-rcna594656",
      "m": "08. 27. PM 01:37",
      "ts": 1787837855000,
      "fullText": "Climate in Crisis Families wait in agony as Nepal floods leaves hundreds missing “It has probably been the worst 24 hours of my life,” Dr. Radhika Sharma, the daughter of one missing American couple, said. Listen to this article with a free profile 00:00 00:00 Add to Google Families await news about missing loves ones after catastrophic floods 00:58 Get more news Live on Share Add to Google Aug. 27, 2026, 9:37 AM EDT By Alexander Smith and Laura Saravia The last time Radhika Sharma heard from her parents, they had just crossed from Nepal into Tibet. Ramesh Sharma, 65, and Neelam Sharma, 64, who live in New York and are U.S. citizens, were making a religious pilgrimage to Mount Kailash, a peak in Tibet considered sacred by Hindus, Buddhists and other faiths. Shortly after their border crossing Wednesday, a towering torrent of water, rock and mud tore through the same Himalayan corridor. Videos from the hardest-hit border regions showed people running as the debris flow swallowed buildings and vehicles, devastated towns and settlements, and severed roads, power and communications. The suspected collapse of a melting glacier shook the ground so violently that seismic monitors initially registered it as a magnitude-4.4 earthquake. In one video from Mailung, in Nepal’s Rasuwa region, a group of construction workers were seen walking down a mountain path before noticing giant plumes of brown debris in the distance, rapidly cascading toward them. The men reversed course to flee uphill, one briefly stopping to film before realizing the scale of the danger. Destruction along the swollen Trishuli River following a massive flash flood, in Nuwakot, Nepal, on Wednesday. Skanda Gautam / SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images He shouts in Nepali: “Oh, my God, the flood is getting so big! Let’s run — run fast, guys! Oh, my God, get back quickly!” By Thursday afternoon, authorities had recovered 289 bodies in Nepal and confirmed three deaths in Tibet. Hundreds more remained unacco"
